  framework (FLAT, a_0 fixed by rho_Lambda):     d log a_0/dz = 0.000 exactly
  LambdaCDM-native emergent RAR scale:           d log a_0/dz = +0.131 (x2.13 by z = 2.5)
  measured, three ways:                          -0.112 (RC100), +0.304 (MSA-3D), +0.295 (MUSE-DARK III)
  wrote /Users/carlzimmerman/new_physics/zimmerman-formula/hunt_2026/h101_rungs.json for item 104

========================================================================================================================
VERDICT (item 101).
  * The closed form runs on THREE of the six named surveys.  KMOS3D, KROSS and MUSE-DARK II tabulate total
    baryonic masses and velocities, not f_DM inside a radius, and are reported rather than faked.
  * SPARC (z~0) and RC100 (z = 0.6-2.5) agree on the LEVEL to 0.07 dex
    (1.18e-10 vs 1.38e-10), which is the one genuinely encouraging number here.
  * MSA-3D sits +0.40 dex away from RC100 in the level (2.2 sigma); MUSE-DARK III, fitting the identical
    kernel directly, is further away still.  Slopes across the three: -0.112 +- 0.064, +0.304 +- 0.309, +0.295 -- opposite signs, but only the
    levels are individually significant; MSA-3D's own slope is 1.0 sigma from zero with N = 23.
  * The disagreement is NOT a mass trend and NOT a redshift trend: inside each survey the inverted a_0 correlates
    with nothing but f_DM itself, and at matched g_bar the two high-z surveys still report f_DM differing by
    +0.18.  It is a disagreement between two dynamical decompositions at the same acceleration.
  * Item 101's pass criterion -- a joint slope separating FLAT from the LambdaCDM-native rise at 5 sigma -- is NOT
    met (1.5 sigma), and the mutation control shows the joint slope is the survey ladder, not redshift.
  * What survives is a hard ceiling on the method: the estimator amplifies the RAR's own residual by A(f_DM) >= 2,
    galaxy by galaxy to r > 0.9, so per-galaxy a_0 from a tabulated f_DM can never beat twice the RAR's scatter.
